  "question": {
    ":question/id": "7acf58b8-f229-5b94-b44a-1370d014e357",
    ":question/problem": "Given that $y=2\\cdot {2}^{-4+4x}$, find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {2}^{u},\\,u=-4+4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}=2\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & =2\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2)\\cdot (4) \\\\ & =(4)\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2) \\\\ & =8\\cdot {2}^{-4+4x}\\ln(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
